@UNIX
Unix-style commands on the TFR/911H (try "man <name>"):
  ls ll cat cp mv rm mkdir rmdir pwd cd ps df less man
  grep clear uname exit     and: tetris zm beep click
Most are aliases that run the OS-9 command shown by "man".
OS-9 paths use / like Unix; /DD is the disk, . and .. work.
Differences: pipes are "!" not "|"; ">>" redirects errors (there is no
append); "<>" and ">>>" redirect several paths; "&" runs in background.
@LS
Syntax: ls [-l] [<path>]   (runs: dir, with -l as -e)
Usage : List a directory.  "ls -l" or "ll" gives the long form.
@LL
Syntax: ll [<path>]        (runs: dir -e)
Usage : List a directory, long form.
@CAT
Syntax: cat <path>         (runs: list)
Usage : Print a text file.
@CP
Syntax: cp <from> <to>     (runs: copy)
Usage : Copy a file.
@MV
Syntax: mv <path> <newname>   (runs: rename)
Usage : Rename a file or directory.  It cannot move it to another directory.
@RM
Syntax: rm <path> ...      (runs: del)
Usage : Delete files.
@MKDIR
Syntax: mkdir <path>       (runs: makdir)
Usage : Make a directory.
@RMDIR
Syntax: rmdir <path>       (runs: deldir)
Usage : Delete a directory and what is in it (it asks first).
@PWD
Syntax: pwd
Usage : Print the data (working) directory.  pxd prints the execution directory.
@CD
Syntax: cd <path>          (Shell built-in, same as chd)
Usage : Change the data (working) directory.  chx changes the execution
        directory, where commands are found.
@PS
Syntax: ps [-e]            (runs: procs)
Usage : List processes.  -e lists everyone's.
@DF
Syntax: df [/dd]           (runs: free)
Usage : Show free space on a disk.  mfree shows free memory.
@LESS
Syntax: less <path>        (runs: more)
Usage : Show a file a screen at a time.
@MAN
Syntax: man [<command>]    (runs: help)
Usage : Show help for a command.  "man unix" lists the Unix-style commands.
@GREP
Syntax: grep [-i] [-n] <pattern> [<path>]
Usage : Print lines containing the pattern (a plain string, not a regexp).
        -i ignores case, -n shows line numbers.  Reads standard input
        if no path is given, so "dir ! grep foo" works.
@CLEAR
Syntax: clear
Usage : Clear the screen.
@UNAME
Syntax: uname
Usage : Say what this is.
@EXIT
Syntax: exit               (Shell built-in)
Usage : Leave this shell.
@TETRIS
Syntax: tetris
Usage : Play Tetris.  Arrows or a/d move, up or w rotates, down or s drops
        a row, space drops the piece, p pauses, q quits, m toggles music.
@ZM
Syntax: zm [-w <cols>] [-h <rows>] [-m <kbytes>] [-d] <story>
Usage : Play an Infocom (or Inform) version 3 story file, such as
        zm zork1.  A bare name is also looked for in /DD/GAMES, and .z3
        may be left off.  -w and -h give the terminal size (80x24); -d is
        for a dumb terminal (no status line).  save and restore ask for a
        file name, in the current data directory.
@BEEP
Syntax: beep
Usage : Ring the bell: the terminal's, and the TFR/911H's speaker.
@CLICK
Syntax: click on|off
Usage : Turn the TFR/911H's keyclicks on or off.
